---
import TrainingLayout from "../../layouts/TrainingLayout.astro";
import Snippet from "../../components/training/Snippet.astro";
import LiveDemo from "../../components/training/LiveDemo.astro";
import ApiLink from "../../components/training/ApiLink.astro";
import ChapterLink from "../../components/training/ChapterLink.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/training/Callout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---
<TrainingLayout slug="formulas">

  <h2 id="idea">Computed, not stored</h2>
  <p>
    Your orders have a quantity and a price. The table should also show the net amount, the gross amount with VAT, the
    share of each order in the total and its rank. You could add these fields to every object – and keep them right after
    every edit, every filter and every new delivery from the server. Or the <strong>column computes them</strong> when a
    cell becomes visible. Since <code>@guiexpert/table@1.1.95</code> there are three tools for that – MIT-licensed:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>calculated columns</strong> – a <code>valueGetter</code> function per column;</li>
    <li><strong>“Show values as”</strong> – a value shown as a share, running total, difference or rank;</li>
    <li><strong>formulas</strong> – a text like <code>ROUND([net] * 1.19, 2)</code> that your users can type.</li>
  </ul>
  <LiveDemo name="formulas" title="1,000 orders – net, gross, share and your own formula" height={460}
            caption="Type a formula into the search field, e.g. ROUND([gross] - [net], 2) or IF([price] > 1000, &quot;big&quot;, &quot;&quot;). Sort by “Share of net”, switch it to a running total or rank, filter Germany." />

  <h2 id="value-getter">valueGetter: one function per column</h2>
  <p>
    <ApiLink name="ColumnDefIf.valueGetter" /> gets the row and returns the value. <code>property</code> stays the id of the
    column – it does not have to exist in the data:
  </p>
  <Snippet file="demos/formulas.ts" region="columns" title="src/order-columns.ts" />
  <p>The important part is <strong>where</strong> the value is used – everywhere the table reads a cell:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>sorting (a click on “Net” sorts by the computed value), copy, Excel export;</li>
    <li>the total row: <code>footerAggregates: {"{ net: \"sum\" }"}</code> sums the computed values
      (<ChapterLink slug="footer-totals" />), row grouping can group and aggregate by them (<ChapterLink slug="row-grouping" />);</li>
    <li>other calculated columns: the second parameter (<ApiLink name="ValueGetterContextIf" />) reads them with
      <ApiLink name="ValueGetterContextIf.getValue" />, column totals over the filtered rows with
      <ApiLink name="ValueGetterContextIf.aggregate" /> (cached).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The filter function gets your row object – read a computed value with <ApiLink name="TableApi.getRowValue" />:
  </p>
  <Code lang="ts" code={`tableOptions.externalFilterFunction = (o: Order) => Number(tableApi.getRowValue(o, "gross")) > 1000;`} />
  <Callout type="note" title="Fast and free of side effects">
    <p>Values are not cached: the getter runs for the visible cells on every render and once per row for a sort or a total.
      A computed column is never editable – unless you give it a <ApiLink name="ColumnDefIf.valueSetter" /> that writes the
      source fields back.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="show-values-as">Show values as: shares, running totals, ranks</h2>
  <p>
    The column “Share of net” has the same value as “Net” – it is only <em>shown</em> differently.
    <ApiLink name="ColumnDefIf.showValuesAs" /> knows seven modes (<ApiLink name="ShowValuesAs" />):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>percentOfColumnTotal</code>, <code>percentOfGrandTotal</code>, <code>percentOfParentTotal</code> (per group
      in a tree or row grouping), <code>percentOfRowTotal</code> (pivot);</li>
    <li><code>runningTotal</code>, <code>differenceFromPrevious</code>, <code>rank</code> (1 = largest).</li>
  </ul>
  <Snippet file="demos/formulas.ts" region="show-as" />
  <p>
    The reference is always the <strong>filtered</strong> rows, like the total row: switch on “only Germany” and the
    visible shares add up to 100 % again. Sorting uses the raw value; Excel gets the shares as numbers with the format
    <code>0.0%</code>. In a pivot table, every value can have its own mode (<ChapterLink slug="pivot-tables" />).
  </p>
  <Snippet file="demos/formulas.ts" region="filter" />

  <h2 id="formulas">Formulas your users can type</h2>
  <p>
    A formula column is a calculated column whose getter is compiled from a text. Fields are written in brackets, the
    functions look like a spreadsheet:
  </p>
  <Code lang="ts" code={`ColumnDef.create({ property: "gross", formula: "ROUND([net] * 1.19, 2)" })
ColumnDef.create({ property: "margin", formula: "IF([net] = 0, 0, ([net] - [cost]) / [net])" })
ColumnDef.create({ property: "share", formula: "ROUND([net] / SUM([net]) * 100, 1)" })   // SUM over the column`} />
  <p>At runtime – e.g. from an input field of your app – <ApiLink name="TableApi.setColumnFormula" /> compiles and repaints:</p>
  <Snippet file="demos/formulas.ts" region="formula" />
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Functions:</strong> <code>ROUND</code>, <code>ABS</code>, <code>IF</code>, <code>IFERROR</code>,
      <code>AND</code>, <code>OR</code>, <code>CONCAT</code>, <code>UPPER</code> … (<ApiLink name="FORMULA_FUNCTION_NAMES" />),
      column aggregates <code>SUM([x])</code>, <code>AVG</code>, <code>MIN</code>, <code>MAX</code>, <code>COUNT</code> –
      and your own functions via <code>formulaOptions: {"{ functions: { VAT: net => Number(net) * 0.19 } }"}</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Errors are values, not exceptions:</strong> <code>#DIV/0!</code>, <code>#NAME?</code> (unknown field),
      <code>#VALUE!</code>, <code>#CIRC!</code> (a column that uses itself) – shown in red with the message as tooltip, sorted
      last, skipped by totals. Test it with <ApiLink name="compileFormula" /> and <ApiLink name="isFormulaError" />.</li>
    <li><strong>Math, not Excel:</strong> <code>-2^2</code> is <code>-4</code>; <code>ROUND</code> rounds half away from zero.</li>
  </ul>
  <Callout type="note" title="Safe with user input">
    <p>Formulas are parsed into a syntax tree and evaluated by a small interpreter – <strong>no <code>eval</code>, no
      <code>new Function</code></strong>, so they work with a strict Content-Security-Policy. Only the listed functions exist,
      fields read own properties only (<code>[constructor]</code> is <code>#NAME?</code>), and Excel gets values, never
      formulas.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="limits">What it is not</h2>
  <p>
    There are no cell references like <code>=B2*C2</code>: with sorting, filtering and virtual scrolling, positions change
    all the time – a formula refers to fields of the same row and to column totals. There is no built-in formula editor;
    the input field belongs to your app. Compare with the alternatives: <ChapterLink slug="build-buy-or-guiexpert" />.
  </p>
  <Callout type="version">
    <p>Since 1.1.95. The tag <code>ep-039</code> of the demo repository follows.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="next">Next</h2>
  <p>Back to the overview of all chapters, or the checklist for your own table: <ChapterLink slug="checklist" />.</p>
</TrainingLayout>
